REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w06","title":"Reading skills: comprehension, methods and tests","summary":"How to read a text so that you understand it, remember it and judge it: the rules for reading comprehension, skimming and scanning, the SQ3R and PQRST methods, reading short and long texts, critical reading (facts and opinions, propaganda, errors in reasoning), and how to plan a timed reading test such as IELTS.","n":6,"lectures":[{"code":"6.1","title":"Tips for reading comprehension","video":"zRJUr8r79IQ","seconds":1196},{"code":"6.2","title":"Reading- skimming & scanning","video":"qby48_n3H_w","seconds":1153},{"code":"6.3","title":"SQ3R and PQRST method","video":"NXnGE0Fu3HA","seconds":1409},{"code":"6.4","title":"Reading shorter & longer texts","video":"jNBOYe26nsc","seconds":1259},{"code":"6.5","title":"Critical Reading | mastering comprehension & analytical reading skills","video":"CYCgkNYtokc","seconds":1152},{"code":"6.6","title":"How to prepare for reading test","video":"8IFuytVl6ZQ","seconds":625}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"why-reading-skills-matter","level":2,"title":"Why reading skills matter"},{"id":"the-rules-for-comprehension","level":2,"title":"The rules for comprehension"},{"id":"comprehension-in-a-test","level":2,"title":"Comprehension in a test"},{"id":"budget-your-time","level":3,"title":"Budget your time"},{"id":"step-1-skim-the-text","level":3,"title":"Step 1: skim the text"},{"id":"step-2-the-easiest-exercises-first","level":3,"title":"Step 2: the easiest exercises first"},{"id":"step-3-read-the-text-and-find-the-answers","level":3,"title":"Step 3: read the text and find the answers"},{"id":"step-4-move-on-to-the-next-exercise","level":3,"title":"Step 4: move on to the next exercise"},{"id":"challenges-and-how-to-improve","level":2,"title":"Challenges and how to improve"},{"id":"critical-reading-facts-propaganda-and-reasoning","level":2,"title":"Critical reading: facts, propaganda and reasoning"},{"id":"1-separate-facts-from-opinion","level":3,"title":"1. Separate facts from opinion"},{"id":"2-detect-propaganda","level":3,"title":"2. Detect propaganda"},{"id":"3-recognise-errors-in-reasoning","level":3,"title":"3. Recognise errors in reasoning"},{"id":"skimming-and-scanning","level":2,"title":"Skimming and scanning"},{"id":"skimming","level":3,"title":"Skimming"},{"id":"scanning","level":3,"title":"Scanning"},{"id":"sq3r","level":2,"title":"SQ3R"},{"id":"s-survey","level":3,"title":"S: Survey"},{"id":"q-question","level":3,"title":"Q: Question"},{"id":"r-read","level":3,"title":"R: Read"},{"id":"r-recite","level":3,"title":"R: Recite"},{"id":"r-review","level":3,"title":"R: Review"},{"id":"pqrst","level":2,"title":"PQRST"},{"id":"shorter-and-longer-texts","level":2,"title":"Shorter and longer texts"},{"id":"shorter-texts","level":3,"title":"Shorter texts"},{"id":"longer-texts","level":3,"title":"Longer texts"},{"id":"critical-reading-the-critical-reader-s-habits","level":2,"title":"Critical reading: the critical reader’s habits"},{"id":"preparing-for-a-reading-test","level":2,"title":"Preparing for a reading test"},{"id":"the-strategies","level":3,"title":"The strategies"},{"id":"how-to-prepare","level":3,"title":"How to prepare"},{"id":"which-way-of-reading-when","level":2,"title":"Which way of reading, when?"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why read</strong> (6.1): reading improves your <strong>vocabulary</strong> and your <strong>grammar</strong>, and these two make your writing and speaking better.</li>\n<li><strong>The rules for comprehension</strong> (6.1): read aloud at times, read with a pen, look over the text quickly first, read the whole paragraph before you highlight, notice <strong>summary words</strong> (<em>therefore, hence, in short</em>), circle keywords and difficult words, <strong>paraphrase</strong>, and question the text.</li>\n<li><strong>Comprehension in a test</strong> (6.1): budget your time; skim; do the easiest exercises first by finding the question’s <strong>keywords or their synonyms</strong> in the passage; read word by word only for the hard ones; guess a hard word from its <strong>context</strong>; never get stuck on one question.</li>\n<li><strong>Critical reading, part 1</strong> (6.1): separate <strong>facts</strong> (they can be proved true with objective evidence) from <strong>opinions</strong> (they cannot); detect <strong>propaganda</strong> (its commonest technique is <strong>transfer</strong>); recognise errors in reasoning (<strong>circular reasoning</strong>, <strong>false cause</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Skimming and scanning</strong> (6.2): <strong>skimming</strong> is a quick look over the whole text for the big picture, “an eagle’s eye view”; <strong>scanning</strong> is a fast search for <strong>one keyword at a time</strong>, then careful reading around it.</li>\n<li><strong>SQ3R and PQRST</strong> (6.3): two five-step methods for reading a chapter so that it stays in your memory. SQ3R = <strong>Survey, Question, Read, Recite, Review</strong> (Francis P. Robinson, 1946). PQRST = <strong>Preview, Question, Read, State (self-recite), Test</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Short and long texts</strong> (6.4): a short text usually has <strong>one key idea</strong>, so give it your full attention. Eat a long text “slice by slice”: chunk it, plan a schedule once you know your pace, and <strong>write notes after every chapter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Critical reading, part 2</strong> (6.5): prepare to receive the text, keep an <strong>open mind</strong>, think about the <strong>title</strong>, learn the <strong>vocabulary and terms</strong>, <strong>write</strong> notes, make <strong>connections</strong> with what you know, and go deep.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading tests</strong> (6.6): IELTS reading has <strong>40 questions in 60 minutes</strong>; spend at most <strong>15, 20 and 25 minutes</strong> on sections 1, 2 and 3; read one question, then scan for its answer; know which question types come <strong>in order</strong>; correct answers are <strong>paraphrased</strong>, and an option that repeats the passage’s exact words is often a trap.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-reading-skills-matter\">Why reading skills matter</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.1 is by Karthika Sathyanathan (Humanities and Social Sciences, IIT Madras). It gives tips to improve your reading, and it starts with a question: why are reading skills important? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=25s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:25</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Reading skills” gives three reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reading improves your vocabulary.</strong> Your <strong>vocabulary</strong> is the set of words you know, and it is the most important thing in learning a language.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading improves your grammar.</strong> When you read actively, you observe the <strong>structure of sentences</strong>: how the words are put together. (<strong>Grammar</strong> is the set of rules for putting words together into sentences.)</li>\n<li><strong>Better vocabulary and grammar lead to better writing and speaking.</strong> This matters most when you use English for <strong>academic</strong> purposes (study) and <strong>business</strong> purposes (work).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading comprehension</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Comprehension</strong> means <em>understanding</em>. <strong>Reading comprehension</strong> is understanding what you read. In an exam, “a reading comprehension” is a <strong>passage</strong> (a short piece of text) followed by questions that test whether you understood it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-rules-for-comprehension\">The rules for comprehension</h2>\n<p>The slide “The rules for comprehension” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=65s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:05</a>) lists eight rules.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The eight rules for comprehension</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read out loud (at times)</strong>: reading and listening.</li>\n<li><strong>Read a book with a pen.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Do a quick scanning</strong> of the book or passage for the important key passages and points.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the entire paragraph</strong> or passage <strong>before you highlight</strong> something.</li>\n<li><strong>Pay attention to summary words</strong>: <em>therefore, as a result, hence, in short, in conclusion</em>, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Circle keywords or difficult jargon</strong> in a passage.</li>\n<li><strong>Paraphrase</strong> what you read.</li>\n<li><strong>Interact and engage with the text</strong>: ask questions, and look for the answers while you read.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>Now each rule, with the reason the lecture gives.</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 1. Read out loud, at times</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). You need not read the whole book aloud: read the <strong>important passages</strong> aloud.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading aloud is <strong>slow reading</strong>. You read with <strong>inflection</strong> (your voice rises and falls) and you follow the <strong>punctuation</strong> (you pause at commas and full stops). So you understand better.</li>\n<li>You also <strong>hear</strong> what you read. Listening is built into reading aloud: you read and listen at the same time. So you grasp the concepts, the keywords and the structure of the sentences better.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rule 2. Read a book with a pen</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Every time you meet a <strong>keyword</strong>, a <strong>summary statement</strong> or an <strong>important passage</strong>, mark it. When you finish the book, you can go straight back to the marked parts.</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 3. Look over the text quickly first</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Do <strong>not</strong> start by reading word by word. First do a quick scan of the book or passage, and find the key terms and the summary statements.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keyword and summary statement</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>keyword</strong> is an important word that tells you what a passage (or a question) is about.</li>\n<li>A <strong>summary statement</strong> is a sentence that gives the main point of a whole paragraph in short.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Rule 4. Read the entire paragraph before you highlight</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). (To <strong>highlight</strong> is to mark words with a pen or a colour.) The first sentence often looks striking, but the summary statement is <strong>not always the first sentence</strong>. Some passages are tricky. So read the whole paragraph, and only then mark its important points.</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 5. Pay attention to summary words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). After these words the author usually gives the conclusion: what the article is really about.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Summary word</th>\n<th>Example sentence (mine)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>therefore</em></td>\n<td>It rained all week; <strong>therefore</strong>, the match was cancelled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>as a result</em></td>\n<td>He practised every day. <strong>As a result</strong>, he won the prize.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>hence</em></td>\n<td>The road was closed; <strong>hence</strong> we took the train.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>in short</em></td>\n<td><strong>In short</strong>, the plan saves both time and money.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>in conclusion</em></td>\n<td><strong>In conclusion</strong>, regular reading builds vocabulary.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Rule 6. Circle keywords and difficult jargon</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Jargon</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Jargon</strong> is the special language of one subject or one profession, which other people find hard to understand (for example, <em>photosynthesis</em> in biology or <em>bandwidth</em> in computing). The lecture uses the word for any difficult or unknown word in a passage.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Circling <strong>keywords</strong> shows you what is <strong>important</strong> in the passage.</li>\n<li>Circling <strong>jargon</strong> shows you what you have <strong>not understood</strong> yet.</li>\n<li>If you do not know a jargon word, read the sentences <strong>before and after</strong> it, and <strong>infer</strong> its meaning from the <strong>context</strong>. The lecture calls this “the only technique” for the problem of jargon (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). There is a worked example in the next section.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Context and infer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>context</strong> of a word is the words and sentences around it. They give clues to its meaning.</li>\n<li>To <strong>infer</strong> is to work out something that is not said directly, from the clues you have.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Jargon</em> has no plural</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture and its slide say “jargons”. In standard English <em>jargon</em> is uncountable, like <em>water</em>: say <em>difficult jargon</em>, <em>a lot of jargon</em>, or <em>jargon words</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Rule 7. Paraphrase what you read</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Paraphrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To <strong>paraphrase</strong> is to say the same idea <strong>in your own words</strong>. If you cannot paraphrase a paragraph (or translate it into your own words), you have not understood it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>For example (mine), the lecture’s sentence “Many educationalists consider it a weak and woolly field” can be paraphrased as “Many experts in education think that this subject is weak and unclear.”</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 8. Interact and engage with the text</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). This means <strong>reading critically</strong>: asking questions while you read, and looking for the answers. The slide’s questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Who is the main character?</li>\n<li>What issue does this article address?</li>\n<li>What is the author trying to tell you?</li>\n<li>Does she make compelling points? (<em>Compelling</em> = strong enough to convince you.)</li>\n<li>Does the author show any bias? (<em>Bias</em> = an unfair preference for one side.)</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember the rules in three groups</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before</strong> you read closely: look over the text quickly (rule 3).</li>\n<li><strong>While</strong> you read: read aloud at times (1), keep a pen in your hand (2), read the whole paragraph before you highlight (4), watch for summary words (5), circle keywords and jargon (6).</li>\n<li><strong>After</strong> you read: paraphrase (7), and question the text (8).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grouping is mine; the rules are the lecture’s.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"comprehension-in-a-test\">Comprehension in a test</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In an exam a passage comes with questions <strong>and a time limit</strong>. The slide “Reading comprehension for tests: tips” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>) gives a plan: first budget your time, then follow a four-step <strong>reading plan</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"budget-your-time\">Budget your time</h3>\n<p>To <strong>budget</strong> your time is to decide in advance how much time each part will get. Ask three questions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How many passages are there?</li>\n<li>How many exercises (sets of questions) must I solve?</li>\n<li>How much time do I have?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then give <strong>less time</strong> to the short or easy passages and <strong>more time</strong> to the difficult or long ones. Time yourself, and stick to the plan.</p>\n<h3 id=\"step-1-skim-the-text\">Step 1: skim the text</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming (lecture 6.1)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Skimming</strong> is “reading the surface of the text to understand the overall message”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>How to skim (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Look at the <strong>heading</strong> and the <strong>subheadings</strong>. They tell you the subject of the text.</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>first few sentences</strong> of each paragraph. The first two lines, or the last two lines, usually <strong>summarise</strong> the paragraph.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming the lecture’s passage on the philosophy of education (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=435s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide says: “Read the first line (marked in red) of the two paras and try to understand the main topic in each paragraph.” Here the red lines are in bold.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Philosophy of Education is a label applied to the study of the purpose, process, nature and ideals of education.</strong> It can be considered a branch of both philosophy and education. Education can be defined as the teaching and learning of specific skills, and the imparting of knowledge, judgment and wisdom, and is something broader than the societal institution of education we often speak of.</p>\n<p><strong>Many educationalists consider it a weak and woolly field, too far removed from the practical applications of the real world to be useful.</strong> But philosophers dating back to Plato and the Ancient Greeks have given the area much thought and emphasis, and there is little doubt that their work has helped shape the practice of education over the millennia.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Read only the first sentence of paragraph 1. It says what the <strong>philosophy of education</strong> is: the study of the purpose, process, nature and ideals of education.</li>\n<li>Read only the first sentence of paragraph 2. It gives a <strong>view about this field</strong>: many educationalists think it is weak and woolly.</li>\n<li>The lecturer calls each first sentence the <strong>summary statement</strong> of its paragraph.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> after only two sentences you know the topic of each paragraph: paragraph 1 defines the philosophy of education, and paragraph 2 is about how people judge it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming gives only a rough idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look at the word <em>But</em> in paragraph 2: the rest of the paragraph <strong>defends</strong> the field (Plato and the ancient Greeks thought about it, and their work shaped education). Skimming gave you the topic, but not this turn. That is why rule 4 says: read the whole paragraph before you highlight. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"step-2-the-easiest-exercises-first\">Step 2: the easiest exercises first</h3>\n<p>After skimming you have a rough idea of the passage. Now go ahead with the <strong>easiest exercises</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read the questions</strong> and understand them fully.</li>\n<li>Find the <strong>keywords</strong> of each question, or their <strong>synonyms</strong> (words with the same meaning). Look for them in the passage: they show you where the answer is.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s true-or-false question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“According to the passage given below, is the following statement True or False? (a) <em>Educationists consider philosophy a ‘weak and woolly’ field.</em>”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Underline the keywords of the question: <strong>educationists</strong> and <strong>weak and woolly field</strong>.</li>\n<li>Search the passage for these words or their synonyms.</li>\n<li>You find them in the fourth line: “Many <strong>educationalists</strong> consider it <strong>a weak and woolly field</strong>, too far removed from the practical applications of the real world to be useful.”</li>\n<li><em>Educationists</em> (in the question) and <em>educationalists</em> (in the passage) are <strong>synonyms</strong>: both mean experts in education. And <em>it</em> refers back to the subject of the passage, the <strong>philosophy of education</strong>.</li>\n<li>The statement and the passage say the same thing, if we read <em>philosophy</em> as short for the <em>philosophy of education</em>, as the lecture does.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> True. (A strict test would notice two differences: the passage speaks of the <em>philosophy of education</em>, not of philosophy in general, and of <em>many</em> educationalists, not all of them. Check such words before you answer.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"step-3-read-the-text-and-find-the-answers\">Step 3: read the text and find the answers</h3>\n<p>Now come to the hard questions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). They need <strong>word-by-word</strong> reading. Move your finger along the text as you read, and you will find the answers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"step-4-move-on-to-the-next-exercise\">Step 4: move on to the next exercise</h3>\n<p>What if you are stuck on a difficult word, or on a question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do <strong>not</strong> spend more time on one question or one difficult word. It can eat up your time.</li>\n<li>For a difficult word, <strong>infer</strong> its meaning from the surrounding information.</li>\n<li>Move on to the next exercise. When you have answered the rest, come back to the difficult question.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Guessing <em>woolly</em> from its context (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The unknown word is <em>woolly</em>.</li>\n<li>Read the whole sentence: “Many educationalists consider it a weak and <strong>woolly</strong> field, too far removed from the practical applications of the real world to be useful.”</li>\n<li>Clue 1: <em>woolly</em> is joined to <em>weak</em> by <em>and</em>, so it is probably close to <em>weak</em> in meaning.</li>\n<li>Clue 2: the rest of the sentence criticises the field: it is “too far removed” from the real world “to be useful”.</li>\n<li>So <em>woolly</em> has a <strong>negative connotation</strong> here. (The <em>connotation</em> of a word is the feeling it carries, besides its basic meaning.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> here <em>woolly</em> means <strong>vague or unclear</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not start with word-by-word reading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Word-by-word reading is for the <strong>hard</strong> questions, after you have skimmed and answered the easy ones. Start word by word and you will run out of time.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"challenges-and-how-to-improve\">Challenges and how to improve</h2>\n<p>All of us have done comprehension passages in exams. In the lecturer’s experience, two problems hold us back (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). The slide: “Challenges while reading: lack of rich vocabulary and practise.” (In British English the noun is <em>practice</em> and the verb is <em>practise</em>, so here it should be <em>practice</em>.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Challenge</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>The cure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of a <strong>rich vocabulary</strong></td>\n<td>You get stuck on difficult words.</td>\n<td>Read widely; note down new words.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of <strong>practice</strong></td>\n<td>You get stuck on questions and <strong>mismanage your time</strong>.</td>\n<td>Practise often.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Deal with these two, and your reading will get better (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “How to improve your reading skills?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read more and read widely.</strong> This is the golden rule. Read as much as possible: newspapers, magazines, books, articles, anything you can get.</li>\n<li><strong>Read something you like in the beginning.</strong> If you are not yet a keen reader, start with topics that interest you: history books if you love history, articles on films if you love films, the sports pages if you like sports, <strong>editorials</strong> (a newspaper’s own opinion articles) if you follow current affairs.</li>\n<li><strong>Read something different every day.</strong> As you progress, read different kinds of material, different <strong>themes</strong> (topics) and different <strong>genres</strong> (types of writing, such as novels, news, essays and poems).</li>\n<li><strong>Note down and review new vocabulary.</strong> Keep a <strong>notebook and a pen</strong> handy while you read. Write down every new word, and review the list later.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice makes reading perfect: read it more than once.</strong> Read as much as possible, and read a text more than once. You get used to the jargon. Then use the new words in your <strong>daily speech</strong> with friends and family (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"critical-reading-facts-propaganda-and-reasoning\">Critical reading: facts, propaganda and reasoning</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The last part of lecture 6.1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). To <strong>read critically</strong> is not to find fault; it is to <strong>judge</strong> what you read. A critical reader asks: what does the author claim, and is the support for it <strong>solid</strong>? The lecture gives three strategies.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"1-separate-facts-from-opinion\">1. Separate facts from opinion</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fact</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>fact</strong> is information that can be <strong>proved true through objective evidence</strong>. Facts can be <strong>checked for accuracy</strong>, and so proved true. (<strong>Objective</strong> evidence is evidence that does not depend on anyone’s feelings, such as data or measurements.)</p>\n<p>The slide’s fact: “China is the most populous country in the world.” It is a fact because a lot of <strong>data</strong> supports it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Opinion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>opinion</strong> is a <strong>belief, judgement or conclusion</strong> that <strong>cannot be objectively proved true</strong>.</p>\n<p>The slide’s opinion: “The best treatment for thyroid is homeopathy.” There is <strong>no consensus</strong> (general agreement) in the scientific community that it is true. Someone else may feel that allopathy, or ayurveda, treats thyroid better (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A critical reader separates the facts from the writer’s opinions. The slide’s note: <strong>much of what we read is a mixture of fact and opinion.</strong></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two questions to tell them apart</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Can it be checked</strong>, with data, records or measurement? A fact can.</li>\n<li><strong>Could a fair person disagree?</strong> Words such as <em>best</em>, <em>worst</em>, <em>beautiful</em> and <em>should</em> usually mark an opinion: people can disagree about them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These two questions are mine; they follow from the lecture’s definitions.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A fact can be checked, and the check can change</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When the lecture was made, China had the largest population. By United Nations estimates, India passed China in 2023. That does not turn the sentence into an opinion: it is still a statement that <strong>data can check</strong>. Today the check says it is no longer true, so a careful writer would now say “India is the most populous country”. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"2-detect-propaganda\">2. Detect propaganda</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Propaganda</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Propaganda</strong> is information, especially of a <strong>biased or misleading</strong> nature, used to <strong>promote a political cause or point of view</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:20</a>). (<em>Biased</em> = unfairly favouring one side; <em>misleading</em> = making you believe something that is not true.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Who uses propaganda the most? <strong>Politicians, advertising agencies and salesmen.</strong> Part of being a critical reader is to recognise propaganda techniques “for the emotional fluff they are”: they work on your <strong>feelings</strong>, not with facts.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Transfer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The most common propaganda technique is <strong>transfer</strong>: a product or a candidate tries to <strong>associate itself with something that people admire or love</strong>, so that the good feeling <strong>transfers</strong> to it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s two examples of transfer by politicians:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>National symbols</strong>, such as the <strong>flag</strong>, to invoke <strong>patriotism</strong> (love of one’s country). The patriotism that the flag stirs in us gets transferred to the politician.</li>\n<li><strong>Famous film actors</strong>: our love and liking for the actor get transferred to the politician.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In both cases our feelings move to the politician, who then gets us to vote for him.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-recognise-errors-in-reasoning\">3. Recognise errors in reasoning</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Valid point and fallacious point</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>valid point</strong> is supported by a <strong>rock-like foundation</strong> of solid support.</li>\n<li>A <strong>fallacious point</strong> is based on a <strong>house of cards</strong> that offers no real support at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>fallacy</strong> is an error in reasoning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Circular reasoning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Circular reasoning</strong> is a fallacy in which <strong>the supporting reason is really the same as the conclusion</strong>. It is illogical: it goes round in a circle and proves nothing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s example of circular reasoning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Mahesh is a great swimmer, because he is so wonderful at swimming.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>conclusion</strong> (what we are asked to accept): Mahesh is a great swimmer.</li>\n<li>The <strong>supporting reason</strong>: he is so wonderful at swimming.</li>\n<li>Compare the two: “a great swimmer” and “wonderful at swimming” say the <strong>same thing</strong> in different words.</li>\n<li>So no real reason has been given. We still do not know <strong>why</strong> he is a great swimmer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> circular reasoning. A real reason would be evidence, for example (mine): “Mahesh is a great swimmer: he has won the state championship twice.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">False cause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>False cause</strong> is a fallacy that <strong>oversimplifies</strong> the issue: it assumes that because event B <strong>follows</strong> event A, event B was <strong>caused</strong> by event A (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s example of false cause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Every day, I eat paratha for breakfast. One time, I had a dosa instead, and there was a major earthquake in my city.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Event A: I ate a dosa instead of a paratha.</li>\n<li>Event B: there was a major earthquake.</li>\n<li>B came <strong>after</strong> A, so the speaker hints that A <strong>caused</strong> B.</li>\n<li>But what we eat for breakfast cannot cause an earthquake. Coming after something is not the same as being caused by it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> false cause. The earthquake did not happen because you had a dosa.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>To sum up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRJUr8r79IQ&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>): critical readers <strong>evaluate an author’s support for a point</strong> and decide whether that support is solid or not. Critical reading needs three abilities: <strong>separating fact from opinion, detecting propaganda, and recognising errors in reasoning</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skimming-and-scanning\">Skimming and scanning</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.2 is by Deepa B Kiran (Humanities and Social Sciences, IIT Madras). Reading can be done in many ways: <strong>detailed reading</strong>, <strong>focused reading</strong>, methods like <strong>SQ3R</strong> and <strong>PQRST</strong>, and others (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Skimming and scanning are for a <strong>large amount of material</strong>, when reading every bit of it with focus would be a laborious (slow and tiring) process, and understanding every word is not the purpose (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). They serve two different outcomes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>to get a sense of the <strong>larger picture</strong>, an <strong>eagle’s eye view</strong> (the way an eagle sees the land from high in the sky);</li>\n<li>to look for <strong>specific information</strong>, and find out whether it is in the text at all.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s picture: a fair with 100 stalls (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You visit a <strong>fair</strong> (a <em>mela</em>, an exhibition) with over 100 stalls.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focused reading</strong> is like going into every stall, one after another, and looking at every item sold there.</li>\n<li><strong>Skimming</strong> is like standing a little away and glancing at all the stalls: clothes here, furniture there, some equipment in that stall, the food corner over there (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). You get a general sense of the fair. Then you decide which stalls to visit, or whether this fair is for you at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Scanning</strong> is like looking for a <strong>friend</strong> who reached the fair before you and is at one of the stalls (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). You glance around (that is skimming), but in the back of your mind there is one specific purpose: to find your friend.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"skimming\">Skimming</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Skimming</strong> is going through a text <strong>very quickly</strong>, without stopping at every word, phrase and sentence, to get a sense of the <strong>larger picture</strong>. It is not for understanding the deeper meaning and the <strong>shades of meaning</strong> (small differences in meaning) of the text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>What to look at when you skim (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>table of contents</strong> and the <strong>index</strong>, to get an idea of the chapters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>);</li>\n<li>the <strong>foreword</strong> (a short piece at the start of a book, often by another writer) and the <strong>introduction</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>headings</strong> of all the chapters, and the <strong>subheadings</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>first paragraph</strong> (the introduction) and the <strong>last paragraph</strong> (the conclusion) of a chapter, and a couple of sentences in between;</li>\n<li>the <strong>starting line</strong> and the <strong>ending line</strong> of each paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>);</li>\n<li><strong>keywords</strong>, and words and phrases that stand out: in <strong>bold</strong>, in <em>italics</em> or underlined;</li>\n<li>the headings of <strong>tables, graphs and illustrations</strong>;</li>\n<li>words or phrases that <strong>repeat</strong>: they show an underlying thread, an idea that runs through the text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>);</li>\n<li>the <strong>summaries</strong> of the chapters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While skimming you may <strong>stop</strong> at a keyword, phrase or sentence that seems significant. Read a few lines <strong>before</strong> it and a few lines <strong>after</strong> it to see what is happening there, and then move on (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<p>When to skim (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>To preview</strong> a text, before deep reading: do I need to go deeper into this text, and if so, into which chapters? You also filter out what has no value for your task. The lecture’s example: for a <strong>600-page</strong> book, skimming can do this job <strong>in an hour instead of half a day</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>To review</strong> a text, after a detailed reading: to get a sense of what you have read (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"scanning\">Scanning</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Scanning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Scanning</strong> is reading with one <strong>specific purpose</strong>: to <strong>locate</strong> the material about a few <strong>keywords</strong> that matter to you, without reading everything else (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>How to scan, step by step (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skim first</strong>, ideally: get a sense of the whole material.</li>\n<li>Decide on the few <strong>keywords</strong> that you want material about.</li>\n<li>Scan for <strong>one keyword at a time</strong>, as you would look for one friend.</li>\n<li>Let your eyes <strong>float</strong> through the whole material, looking only for that keyword.</li>\n<li>When you find it, <strong>stop</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Read a little <strong>before</strong> and a little <strong>after</strong> that place. Go deeper there, not into the whole text.</li>\n<li>Repeat with the next keyword.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecture compares scanning to <strong>mining</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>): there is a lot of material, but not all of it means something to you. You find and dig out only what does.</p>\n<p><strong>Scanning to answer a question</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>): first read the question well, and know its keywords and what the answer needs. Then look for those keywords in the text, and pause when you find them. Look not only at the text but also at the <strong>illustrations and tables</strong>. Skim first, to see the cues and how the material is organised; then scan to zero in on exactly what you need (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Choose your time and place for scanning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Scanning needs a lot of <strong>focus and concentration</strong>. It can feel overwhelming and drain your energy. Choose a place <strong>without distractions</strong>, and a time when you feel <strong>full of energy</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qby48_n3H_w&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Skimming</th>\n<th>Scanning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>the overall picture</td>\n<td>one specific piece of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You look for</td>\n<td>headings, first and last lines, bold words, summaries</td>\n<td>one keyword at a time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At the fair</td>\n<td>glance at all the stalls</td>\n<td>look for your friend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use it</td>\n<td>to preview or review a text</td>\n<td>to locate a fact or an answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Order</td>\n<td>first</td>\n<td>after skimming</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Scan” in lecture 6.1</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Rule 3 of lecture 6.1 says “do a quick <strong>scanning</strong> of the passage” for its key points. There the word means a quick look over the whole text, which is close to what lecture 6.2 calls <strong>skimming</strong>. Keep the two exact meanings of lecture 6.2: <strong>skim</strong> for the big picture, <strong>scan</strong> for a specific keyword.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"sq3r\">SQ3R</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.3 is by Deepa B Kiran. SQ3R and PQRST are <strong>acronyms</strong>: names made from the first letters of the steps. Both methods help you <strong>gain meaningfully</strong> from what you read, so that your reading is effective and grows your knowledge. Practise a method regularly and it becomes a <strong>habit</strong>: each time you open a text, you automatically read in the more effective way (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The closing slide: “The information we collect from a written text is important. The SQ3R reading method helps to gain as much information and as deeply and clearly as possible in the process of reading” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">SQ3R</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>SQ3R</strong> is a reading comprehension method with <strong>five steps</strong>: the <strong>S</strong>, the <strong>Q</strong> and the <strong>three R’s</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>S</strong>urvey</li>\n<li><strong>Q</strong>uestion</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ead</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ecite (the closing slide also calls it “tell-aloud”)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>eview</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It was introduced by <strong>Francis P. Robinson</strong>, an American educationalist and philosopher, in his book <em><strong>Effective Study</strong></em>, which came out in <strong>1946</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"s-survey\">S: Survey</h3>\n<p>A <strong>survey</strong>, in general, is an overall look at something, in which you identify its elements and features for a study. Surveying a text means getting a sense of the <strong>whole text</strong> before you read it: what are its elements, and what does it offer you as a reader? This <strong>prepares</strong> you to receive the text well (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s picture: going to a film (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Friends ask you to watch a film with them. If you know that it is a horror film, a period film about a historical conflict, or a romantic film, you prepare your mind for that kind of experience. If you also know the director (and have seen the director’s other films), the writer or the production house, you are even better prepared (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). A survey does the same for a text.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>What to survey (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>title</strong>: what does it tell you about the author, the text and its approach? What areas might the text cover? How long or brief will it be?</li>\n<li>the <strong>introduction</strong>, the <strong>foreword</strong>, other notes at the start, and <strong>reviews</strong>: they help you make sense of the chapters, their order and their focus (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>);</li>\n<li><strong>textual indicators</strong>: the titles of the chapters, and the <strong>headings and subheadings</strong> within chapters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>);</li>\n<li><strong>illustrations</strong>: maps, diagrams, flowcharts, sketches, photographs;</li>\n<li><strong>textual aids</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>): <strong>footnotes</strong> (notes at the foot of a page), words in <em>italics</em>, items marked with an <strong>asterisk</strong> (*), short explanations or <strong>commentary</strong> beside the text, and the <strong>bibliography</strong> (the list of books the author used).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A survey is like looking at the <strong>map of the terrain</strong> before you walk into it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"q-question\">Q: Question</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s picture: a stone and a sponge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Pour water on a stone, and the water flows away. Pour water on a sponge, and the sponge absorbs it. A <strong>curious mind</strong> is like the sponge: “a curious mind is a learning mind”, and it soaks in information naturally and easily.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The way to switch into this curious, learning mode is to <strong>ask questions</strong>. Turn every chapter title, every heading and every subheading into a <strong>question</strong>, and notice the questions and curiosities that come up as you read (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<p>For example (mine), the heading <em>Causes of the First World War</em> becomes the question <em>What caused the First World War?</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"r-read\">R: Read</h3>\n<p><strong>Chunk</strong> the material: divide it into <strong>sections</strong>, small “bite-sized” parts that you can handle, and read <strong>part by part</strong>. As you read each part, see whether you can <strong>fill in the answers</strong> to the questions in your mind, and notice what else you learn (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"r-recite\">R: Recite</h3>\n<p><strong>Recite</strong> is about <strong>recall</strong>: bringing something back from memory (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>As soon as you finish a section, try to <strong>answer</strong> all your questions from memory, and <strong>say the answers back</strong>.</li>\n<li>If you cannot, go back to the text, <strong>check</strong>, and then recite again.</li>\n<li>Do <strong>not</strong> go further until you can.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Why? You are training your mind to <strong>concentrate</strong> and to <strong>store</strong> the information. You are also ready for the next part, because the author wrote it assuming that you have understood what came before (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"r-review\">R: Review</h3>\n<p>In everyday use, a <strong>review</strong> comes when something is over: a film review, a book review, a song review, a review of a hotel or of a meal. In SQ3R you review the <strong>entire chapter</strong> after you finish it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Look back at <strong>all the questions</strong>.</li>\n<li>See how many of them you can recall and answer, and how well (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Get a general sense of the <strong>whole chapter</strong>.</li>\n<li>Wherever you cannot answer, go back, check, and refresh your memory (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Only when the whole chapter makes sense to you and is well registered in your mind, go ahead to the next one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Recite or review?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Recite</strong> after each <strong>section</strong>: small and frequent. <strong>Review</strong> after the whole <strong>chapter</strong>: big, at the end.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"pqrst\">PQRST</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">PQRST</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>PQRST</strong> is a reading method with <strong>five steps</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>P</strong>review</li>\n<li><strong>Q</strong>uestion</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ead</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>tate, or <strong>S</strong>elf-recite</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>est (the closing slide adds “evaluate”)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The closing slide: it is a method “that helps the reader to understand the organization of the written text and to analyze it deeply for meaningful reading with an effective outcome” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The five steps help you read in an <strong>organised</strong> way. Research has shown that with PQRST you <strong>retain</strong> information (keep it in memory) more strongly and deeply (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Of the five steps, the <strong>first and the last</strong> (<strong>Preview</strong> and <strong>Test</strong>) are for the <strong>entire chapter</strong>; the <strong>middle three</strong> (<strong>Question, Read, State</strong>) are for <strong>each section</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>P: Preview</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). Like a film’s preview, a preview gives you a sense of what the whole, longer work is about. Look quickly over the chapter: its title, the subheadings, the illustrations (flowcharts, tables, diagrams, graphs, photographs), highlighted keywords and footnotes.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Question</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Keep one good question in mind: <em>What point is the author making in this section, or in this chapter? What am I expected to learn from it?</em> When this question is your pivot (the centre around which everything turns), you are more likely to understand.</p>\n<p><strong>R: Read</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). Read with <strong>intention</strong>: to understand the key ideas, to retain them and to remember them. Read consciously and carefully, and <strong>connect</strong> what you read with what you already know about the same topic and about related topics. Underline, mark keywords and make small notes if it helps, but in moderation.</p>\n<p><strong>S: State, or self-recite</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>When you finish a section, sit back.</li>\n<li>Recall the <strong>main ideas</strong>, the <strong>key concepts</strong> and the information you gathered.</li>\n<li><strong>Say it out loud</strong> (but think about where you are first!).</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-check</strong> with the book: is it what you thought?</li>\n<li>Answer the questions you asked yourself before reading the section (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecture’s picture: when a friend asks you a doubt about a chapter and you explain it, the chapter becomes much clearer to you, and it goes deeper into your memory. In this step you <strong>explain it to yourself</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>T: Test</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). “That which you measure grows.” Testing is <strong>measuring</strong> (evaluating) what you have learned and how you have grown. Do it <strong>fresh</strong>, just after you finish the chapter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Review the whole chapter.</li>\n<li>Answer all your questions again, and check whether the answers are in your memory.</li>\n<li>Wherever they are not, go back and <strong>reread</strong>.</li>\n<li>Look at the <strong>summaries</strong>; write them out again if needed.</li>\n<li>Test how much you have understood, and how many of your questions got answered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Research shows that PQRST makes you a more meaningful reader, both in <strong>comprehending</strong> what you read and in <strong>recollecting</strong> it later (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXnGE0Fu3HA&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>SQ3R</th>\n<th>PQRST</th>\n<th>Done for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Survey</td>\n<td>Preview</td>\n<td>the whole chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Read</td>\n<td>Read</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Recite (tell aloud)</td>\n<td>State (self-recite)</td>\n<td>each section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>Test (evaluate)</td>\n<td>the whole chapter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The last column is the lecture’s rule for PQRST; the SQ3R steps fall the same way (recite after each section, review after the chapter).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One idea under two names</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The two methods share five ideas: <strong>look over</strong> (Survey or Preview), <strong>ask</strong> (Question), <strong>read</strong> (Read), <strong>say it back</strong> (Recite or State), <strong>check yourself</strong> (Review or Test).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"shorter-and-longer-texts\">Shorter and longer texts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.4 is by Deepa B Kiran. On the mobile phone, “the shorter the better” seems to be the way we are heading: acronyms, summaries, bullet points, crunched ideas (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Even fiction has <strong>micro stories</strong>: 6-word stories and 10-word stories (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). People say that attention spans are getting shorter. If we accept that, shorter texts look easier: they take less time, focus and effort, they finish fast, and we remember them more easily. That is true to quite an extent (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Texts can be <strong>fiction</strong> (invented stories) or <strong>non-fiction</strong> (about real facts and ideas). Short texts in literature are <strong>short stories, poems and essays</strong>. There are also short scientific texts on history, geography, information technology, biochemistry and so on (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"shorter-texts\">Shorter texts</h3>\n<p>The closing slide gives the characteristics of shorter texts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>They can be completed <strong>quickly</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Remembering</strong> them is more likely.</li>\n<li>You can <strong>concentrate</strong> and give attention to <strong>details</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>What is a short text for? Usually it presents <strong>one simple, small key idea</strong>, and supports it with relevant examples and explanations, sometimes with exercises, activities and worksheets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“It is short, so no big deal”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A short text is easier to approach, but it still needs <strong>alertness and focus</strong>. If you are laid back about it, you can get caught out (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s picture: a child with a chocolate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A grown-up unwraps a chocolate, pops it into the mouth while talking to someone, and is half eating it and half lost in the conversation. A child sits down, unwraps the chocolate, and enjoys every bit of every bite. Read a short text like the child: give it <strong>all your focus, attention and curiosity</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Questions to ask while you read a short text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the author saying in this space, and <strong>how</strong>?</li>\n<li>What does the author give to <strong>substantiate</strong> (support with evidence) the key idea? Is it done cleverly or not? That is for you to observe.</li>\n<li>What is the author doing to help me, the reader, understand the key idea better?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then put down small notes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the <strong>key idea</strong>?</li>\n<li>How was it <strong>introduced</strong>?</li>\n<li>How was it <strong>substantiated</strong>?</li>\n<li>How was it <strong>concluded</strong>?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A short text also gives you a sense of <strong>accomplishment</strong>: “I have done it.” So short texts are good for your <strong>confidence</strong>. If you feel anxious and not fully confident, read a short text first, finish it, feel more confident, and then go on to the longer text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"longer-texts\">Longer texts</h3>\n<p>The closing slide gives the characteristics of longer texts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>They can be <strong>overwhelming</strong> in terms of <strong>volume</strong>: there is a lot of them.</li>\n<li>Completing them is a <strong>long-drawn</strong> process.</li>\n<li>They are <strong>not easy to recollect</strong>.</li>\n<li>They can be <strong>more difficult to comprehend</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Longer literary texts</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): novels, biographies, philosophical works.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading <strong>for leisure</strong> (for pleasure), you can enjoy the text, stop, <strong>mull</strong> (think slowly), and reflect on the beauty of the words and ideas and on their connections with your own life. It is like taking a walk.</li>\n<li>Reading <strong>for the curriculum</strong> (for your course), you also make notes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). For <strong>fiction</strong>: the plot, the characters, the framework, the progression and sequence of the story, how each character is defined and grows, the frictions and relations between the characters, and the situations that cause them. For <strong>biographies</strong>: the journey of the <strong>protagonist</strong> (the main character). For <strong>philosophy</strong>: reflect on your own life, your thought processes and your relationships (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Longer concept texts</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>): a “big fat textbook” on biotechnology, genetics, mathematics or artificial intelligence (or a digital file that takes up a lot of memory). For some readers a big book is overwhelming.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s quotation: eating an elephant (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“How do you eat an elephant? You eat it slice by slice.”</p>\n<p>(The lecturer adds that she is not recommending eating elephants!) When something seems too big to handle, <strong>chunk it down</strong>: break it up into bits and pieces (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A long text is usually already broken into <strong>chapters</strong>, which makes life easy: the foreword and the introduction today, chapter 1 tomorrow, chapter 2 the day after. You might finish a long book in 21 days at one or two chapters a day; that is better than not reading it at all (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). So a longer text is really <strong>a set of short texts put together</strong>. Each time you finish a chapter, give yourself a pat on the back: it builds your confidence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Plan a schedule</strong>, especially for a longer text. But how many chapters a day? The lecture tells a story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s story: the old woman and the temple</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A young boy reaches a new town. He wants to visit the temple at the top of a small hill.</li>\n<li>He asks an old woman at a stall nearby: “Amma, how long to reach the temple at the top?”</li>\n<li>She looks at him and goes on with her work. The boy thinks, “How rude of her!”, and starts climbing.</li>\n<li>After a while she shouts: “15 minutes!”</li>\n<li>The boy shouts back: “Why didn’t you tell me when I asked you?”</li>\n<li>She says: “I had to see at what pace you walk.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The lesson:</strong> read a chapter or two first. Then you know <strong>your own rhythm and pace</strong> with this text, and you can plan the rest: one chapter a day or two, and how many hours. You find this out only by “getting your feet wet”, by actually reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Make notes</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). With a short text you can sometimes manage without notes. With a long text, writing at the <strong>end of each chapter</strong> is “almost an essential must” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>): highlights, comments, doodles or <strong>sketch notes</strong> (notes made of small drawings and words), whatever works for you. The text is long, so there is much more to remember. By chapter 4 we have probably forgotten what we read in chapter 1, and authors often refer back to earlier chapters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<p>What to note at the end of each chapter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>): the <strong>key words</strong>, specific <strong>vocabulary</strong>, <strong>definitions</strong> of terms, the <strong>main concepts and ideas</strong>, the <strong>examples</strong>, important <strong>diagrams and formulas</strong>, <strong>dates</strong>: whatever is critical. Together these make comprehensive notes that are easy to refer to later.</p>\n<p>Writing notes also shows you <strong>what you have understood and what you have not</strong>. Something seems clear (“Yes, I got this”) until you try to write it down <strong>in your own words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Feynman’s test (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Richard Feynman</strong>, the famous physicist and Nobel laureate, said: if you have learned a concept but <strong>cannot explain it to a child in the fifth class</strong> without hiding behind big, big terms, then perhaps you have not understood it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>One part of learning is to read texts like this. Another, more important part is to be able to <strong>communicate and articulate</strong> (express clearly) what you have learned (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). Good notes may need you to go back and forth in the text, revise, look things up in a <strong>thesaurus</strong> (a book of words grouped by meaning) or on Google, and ask friends, classmates, teachers or colleagues (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<p>What you gain (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>): you harvest the text fully; you have clarity and comprehension; you remember better, and whatever you forget you can pull out of your <strong>notes file</strong>. By the end you have strung together the key ideas of each chapter, with their supporting metaphors, examples, explanations and definitions: <strong>your own draft version of the author’s text</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). You also see how a writer’s mind works. Practised regularly, this becomes a habit and boosts your confidence as a reader (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Things to keep in mind while reading longer texts</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The closing slide’s seven points (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNBOYe26nsc&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Try to <strong>restrain</strong> yourself from getting <strong>overwhelmed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Break it down</strong> and chunk it down into bits.</li>\n<li>Plan out a <strong>schedule</strong>: chapter-wise or number-of-pages-wise reading.</li>\n<li><strong>Make notes</strong>, highlight, add comments.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify and note</strong>: keywords, difficult vocabulary, important terms and phrases, significant diagrams, etc.</li>\n<li>Understand and go over the <strong>important sections</strong> of the text <strong>multiple times</strong>, to ensure comprehension.</li>\n<li>Pull out the <strong>key concept</strong> in each chapter, and make notes for it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Thus simplify the process, improve comprehension and increase <strong>recall value</strong>” (how well you can remember it later).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Shorter text</th>\n<th>Longer text</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>one key idea</td>\n<td>many chapters, many ideas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How it feels</td>\n<td>quick, easy to remember</td>\n<td>overwhelming, long-drawn, hard to recollect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The danger</td>\n<td>being laid back</td>\n<td>giving up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How to read</td>\n<td>full focus, like a child with a chocolate</td>\n<td>slice by slice, on a schedule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notes</td>\n<td>key idea; how introduced, supported, concluded</td>\n<td>after every chapter: key words, terms, concepts, diagrams</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"critical-reading-the-critical-reader-s-habits\">Critical reading: the critical reader’s habits</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.5 is by Deepa B Kiran. Not all reading is critical reading. A glance at a billboard (an advertisement or a film poster) from a taxi in traffic is a quick look, and that is fine (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). <strong>Critical reading</strong> happens when we sit down with a specific text and <strong>consciously invest effort</strong>. The goal is not just to finish the text, but to get <strong>clarity</strong> about its <strong>content</strong>: what the text says and <strong>how</strong> its ideas are presented, and to comprehend it fully (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Some of this comes <strong>naturally</strong>: when the topic interests you, when you already have many reference points, or when the text makes you curious. But you can also do it <strong>consciously</strong>, with the habits below (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Critical reading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The closing slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=1130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:50</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Critical reading involves <strong>conscious and focused effort</strong>,</li>\n<li>following certain <strong>processes and methods</strong>,</li>\n<li>with a clear <strong>intent and determination to understand the text in depth</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>1. Prepare to receive the text</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Before you read critically, become better informed about the <strong>author</strong> (or authors): read a little about them and about their other works. For this work itself, read the references, the introduction, the notes at the start and the foreword. It is like <strong>tuning a radio</strong> to the right frequency: you tune in to the channel of this author and this topic (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>2. Go with an open mind</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s quotation: the parachute (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The parachute has a smooth landing only when it opens; it is going to crash-land otherwise.” A mind, like a parachute, works only when it is open.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Reading with an open mind means (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>keep aside your prior knowledge, your <strong>subjectivity</strong> (your personal feelings and views), your judgement and your evaluation;</li>\n<li>do not edit and rewrite the text in your head while you read it;</li>\n<li><strong>acknowledge</strong> what is written there, and give the author his or her <strong>due worth</strong>;</li>\n<li>do not write the text off, dismiss it, or be <strong>condescending</strong> towards it (look down on it).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The closing slide puts it as: be open and receptive to “new” knowledge and to <strong>repackaged old knowledge</strong> (old ideas presented in a new way).</p>\n<p><strong>3. Dwell on the title</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). (To <strong>dwell on</strong> something is to think about it for a while.) As a child you met this idea: the teacher showed you a book’s cover, asked you to read the title, and asked you to <strong>predict</strong> what the book is about. Do the same now (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What does the title say? Why has it been said?</li>\n<li>Why might the author have chosen <strong>these words and not others</strong>?</li>\n<li>What might the title tell you about the author’s personality, pursuits, approach and perspective?</li>\n<li>What kind of work does the <strong>publishing house</strong> do (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The closing slide also lists the <strong>tag line</strong> (a short line under the title), the <strong>index</strong> and the <strong>chapter headings</strong>. Thinking about all these expands your range as a critical reader.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Understand the vocabulary and the terminology</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). (<strong>Terminology</strong> is the set of special terms of a subject.) The same word can mean something quite different in a <strong>legal</strong>, a <strong>medical</strong> or a <strong>data science</strong> context. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>stop</strong> at tough words and at terms you do not understand; do not jump over them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>);</li>\n<li>look up their meanings and definitions <strong>before</strong> you go further: search online, or use a dictionary such as <strong>Merriam-Webster</strong>, or a thesaurus;</li>\n<li>note them down, and cross-check with a peer, a colleague or a teacher (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sometimes a term becomes clear as you read on. But a critical reader stops and puts in the extra effort.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Put it down in writing</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Learners once rewrote the whole text they were reading. We do not do that now, but it is still recommended to <strong>write about what you read</strong>. An author puts down ideas and supports each one with examples, proof and explanations, and usually introduces each idea and then summarises it. Some authors show this clearly: an introduction, the points, a conclusion, even a bullet-point summary (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). As a critical reader, look for all of this. Ask yourself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What exactly does the author want to say here? What is the <strong>one critical idea</strong>, or what are the <strong>two or three main ideas</strong>?</li>\n<li>How is the author <strong>supporting</strong> the ideas? What <strong>examples</strong> and <strong>explanations</strong> are given?</li>\n<li>How are the ideas <strong>linked</strong>, one to the next?</li>\n<li>Which words, terms, phrases or sentences are so important that I do not want to forget them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Underline, highlight or write small comments, whatever works for you. Then gather it all as <strong>bullet points, notes, a mind map, sketch notes or doodles</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). Some people explain it all to a friend or a peer afterwards. Writing does the same job on your own: “you are literally talking to your book” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Two reasons to write (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Writing <strong>seals the learning</strong> much deeper.</li>\n<li>Next time you need not read the whole text again: you have your own <strong>summary and notes</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>6. Make connections</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). After reading, notice the “dots” in your mind that connect with the text: what you studied before about the same subject, and links to other areas of knowledge, whether <strong>personal, social, political or historical</strong>. The closing slide: make connections and references with your own <strong>knowledge base</strong>: life experiences, socio-political understanding, technical background in the subject, your knowledge of another subject or of a related subject. This gives you a rich <strong>360-degree perspective</strong>, which is valued today because <strong>interdisciplinary</strong> learning (learning that joins several subjects) is encouraged (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>7. Go deep.</strong> The closing slide adds: “Read to get not only the <strong>eagle’s eye view</strong> but go deep into the text and the topic” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=1130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:50</a>). Skimming gives the eagle’s eye view; critical reading goes further.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Things you could do to be a critical reader</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The closing slide’s seven points (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=1130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:50</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prepare to receive the written works on this topic and by this author.</li>\n<li>Be open and receptive to “new” knowledge and repackaged old knowledge.</li>\n<li>Reflect on the title, tag line, index and chapter headings.</li>\n<li>Take the help of a dictionary and any other technical support.</li>\n<li>Write down notes and points.</li>\n<li>Make connections and references with your own knowledge base.</li>\n<li>Read to get not only the eagle’s eye view but go deep into the text and the topic.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The spoken summary lists the first six (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). Critical reading is <strong>slow, focused and conscious</strong>, and it takes effort. But the results are deeply meaningful: they improve not only your reading but also your <strong>writing</strong>, and all the knowledge you build as a learner or a professional (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CYCgkNYtokc&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two lectures, one skill</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.1 says <strong>what to judge</strong> in a text: fact or opinion? propaganda? sound reasoning? Lecture 6.5 says <strong>how to work</strong> as a critical reader: prepare, keep an open mind, think about the title, learn the terms, write, connect, go deep. Use both together.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"preparing-for-a-reading-test\">Preparing for a reading test</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.6 is by Karthika Sathyanathan. It uses the <strong>IELTS</strong> exam as its example. (IELTS, the International English Language Testing System, is an English test taken by people who want to study or work abroad.) But the strategies work for <strong>any reading test</strong> in which you read passages and answer questions on them: the GRE, the TOEFL, or the reading part of any other test (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">IELTS reading: the exam format</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Exam format” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=20s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:20</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>60 minutes</strong> and <strong>40 questions</strong>.</li>\n<li>There are two exams: <strong>Academic</strong> and <strong>General</strong> (General Training).</li>\n<li>For the Academic test, there are <strong>three sections</strong>, with <strong>one long passage</strong> per section.</li>\n<li>For General Training, usually two passages in the first two sections, and one passage in the third section.</li>\n<li>Overall, the questions of the General Training exam are <strong>easier</strong> than those of the Academic exam.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Band scores</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=105s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:45</a>). IELTS gives your result as a <strong>band score</strong>, on a scale up to <strong>9</strong>, in steps of <strong>0.5</strong>. The slide “Band score and the number of correct answers” shows how many of the 40 answers must be correct for each band:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band score</th>\n<th>Academic: correct answers</th>\n<th>General: correct answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>39–40</td>\n<td>40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8.5</td>\n<td>37–38</td>\n<td>39</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>35–36</td>\n<td>37–38</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7.5</td>\n<td>33–34</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>30–32</td>\n<td>34–35</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6.5</td>\n<td>27–29</td>\n<td>32–33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>23–26</td>\n<td>30–31</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.5</td>\n<td>19–22</td>\n<td>27–29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15–18</td>\n<td>23–26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.5</td>\n<td>13–14</td>\n<td>19–22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>10–12</td>\n<td>15–18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3.5</td>\n<td>8–9</td>\n<td>12–14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6–7</td>\n<td>9–11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>4–5</td>\n<td>6–8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>How to read the table: for band 9, the Academic test needs 39 or 40 correct answers, but the General test needs all 40. In every row the General test needs <strong>more</strong> correct answers for the same band, because its questions are easier.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-strategies\">The strategies</h3>\n<p>The slides “Strategies” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=195s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:15</a>):</p>\n<p><strong>1. Easy questions first, and fast.</strong> The reading section goes from <strong>simple</strong> questions to <strong>difficult</strong> ones: the further you go, the harder they get. So go through the easy questions quite quickly, to save time for the difficult ones.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Budget the 60 minutes</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>Difficulty</th>\n<th>Time, at most</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>easy</td>\n<td>15 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>easy to medium</td>\n<td>20 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>the toughest</td>\n<td>25 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>15 + 20 + 25 = 60 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>3. Do not spend too much time on one question</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). It is common to get stuck on a question whose answer you cannot find, and many candidates make this mistake. If you spend the time meant for 4 or 5 questions on a single question, you lose the marks of 4 or 5 questions, even if you get that one right. So <strong>mark</strong> the question, move on, and come back to it if you have extra time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why one stuck question costs so much (my arithmetic, with the lecture’s numbers)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Average time per question: 60 minutes ÷ 40 questions = 1.5 minutes.</li>\n<li>Time for 4 questions: 4 × 1.5 = 6 minutes. Time for 5 questions: 5 × 1.5 = 7.5 minutes.</li>\n<li>Spend 6 to 7.5 minutes on one hard question, and 4 or 5 other questions may go unanswered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> you risk losing 4 or 5 marks to gain at most 1. Mark it and move on.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>4. Never read the whole passage in one go</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). Many questions come <strong>in order</strong>. So:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the <strong>first question</strong>.</li>\n<li>Quickly <strong>scan</strong> the passage until you find where the answer is.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow down</strong>: reread the question and the sentence, and find the answer.</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>second question</strong>, and repeat.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This method saves a lot of time.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Know which questions come in order</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ordered and non-ordered questions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ordered</strong> questions follow the order of the passage: the answer to the first question comes earliest, and the answer to the last question comes latest. If the answer to question 1 is in paragraph 2, the answer to question 2 is in paragraph 2 or after it, <strong>never</strong> in paragraph 1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Non-ordered</strong> questions can have their answers anywhere, so you must read or scan the <strong>whole passage</strong> for each one.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide’s list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=355s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:55</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question type</th>\n<th>In order?</th>\n<th>Where to look</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing words</td>\n<td>ordered</td>\n<td>from the previous answer on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>True/False and Yes/No/Not given</td>\n<td>ordered</td>\n<td>from the previous answer on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes/No/Not given <strong>in section 3</strong></td>\n<td>not ordered</td>\n<td>the whole passage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple choice</td>\n<td>not ordered</td>\n<td>the whole passage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Match the following</td>\n<td>paragraph number given</td>\n<td>just that paragraph</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Matching paragraphs (“which paragraph contains …”)</td>\n<td>not ordered</td>\n<td>the whole passage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentence completion</td>\n<td>not ordered</td>\n<td>the whole passage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>All</strong> questions in section 3</td>\n<td>not ordered</td>\n<td>the whole passage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So section 3 is the toughest: none of its questions come in order, and the slide calls them the most difficult (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The official descriptions differ</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The IELTS partners’ own descriptions of the question types do not match this list in every row. They say the answers come <strong>in the same order as the text</strong> for <strong>multiple choice</strong>, <strong>sentence completion</strong> and <strong>short-answer</strong> questions, and usually for True/False/Not given and Yes/No/Not given. They say the answers are <strong>not necessarily in order</strong> for <strong>summary, note and table completion</strong> (gap-fill), <strong>matching information</strong> and <strong>diagram labels</strong>. The order depends on the question type, not on the section. For this course’s exam, know the lecture’s list; for a real IELTS test, go by the official one. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-prepare\">How to prepare</h3>\n<p>The last “Strategies” slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Take a lot of practice tests</strong> before the exam. Get used to the exam format and the question types.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify the keywords</strong> of each question. They keep you concentrated on what you are looking for. <strong>Highlight</strong> them, and also key information such as <strong>dates and names</strong>.</li>\n<li>The answers to most questions lie in <strong>several words</strong>, rarely in one word. You do not need to understand everything: you just need to find those words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Expect paraphrase.</strong> The correct answers and the words of the key phrase will be <strong>paraphrased</strong>: they will not be repeated, but the meaning will be the same. If you find the <strong>same words</strong> in the text and in one of the options, it is most likely a <strong>trap</strong>, not the correct option. So check the <strong>meaning</strong>; do not fix your eyes on the keyword alone (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8IFuytVl6ZQ&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How a paraphrase trap works (my example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Passage: “Tickets used to be sold at the gate, but since 2020 visitors must book online.”</p>\n<p>Question: How do visitors get tickets now? (a) They buy them at the gate. (b) They reserve them on the internet.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The keywords of the question: <em>tickets</em> and <em>now</em>.</li>\n<li>Scan the passage for <em>tickets</em>: only one sentence mentions them.</li>\n<li>Option (a) repeats the passage’s exact words <em>at the gate</em>, but the passage says that was the <strong>old</strong> way (“used to be”).</li>\n<li>Option (b) uses new words, <em>reserve</em> (= book) and <em>on the internet</em> (= online), with the <strong>same meaning</strong> as the passage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b). The option with the passage’s exact words was the trap.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The whole lecture in one line</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Easy questions fast; 15, 20 and 25 minutes; mark a stuck question and move on; one question, then scan; know the ordered types; look for the same <strong>meaning</strong>, not the same <strong>words</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"which-way-of-reading-when\">Which way of reading, when?</h2>\n<p>The week gives you several ways to read. Choose by your purpose:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Your purpose</th>\n<th>Read this way</th>\n<th>Lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>See the big picture; decide what to read</td>\n<td>skim</td>\n<td>6.1, 6.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find one fact, or the answer to a question</td>\n<td>scan</td>\n<td>6.2, 6.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Answer a hard question</td>\n<td>word by word, finger on the line</td>\n<td>6.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Study a chapter and remember it</td>\n<td>SQ3R or PQRST</td>\n<td>6.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finish a big book</td>\n<td>chunk, schedule, notes after each chapter</td>\n<td>6.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Judge whether a text is fair and well argued</td>\n<td>critical reading</td>\n<td>6.1, 6.5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why read</strong>: reading builds vocabulary and grammar, which make writing and speaking better.</li>\n<li><strong>Eight rules</strong>: read aloud at times; read with a pen; look over the text first; read the whole paragraph before you highlight; notice summary words (<em>therefore, as a result, hence, in short, in conclusion</em>); circle keywords and jargon, and infer meanings from context; paraphrase; engage with the text (main character? issue? the author’s message? compelling? biased?).</li>\n<li><strong>In a test</strong>: budget your time (less for short or easy passages, more for long or hard ones); skim (headings, first lines); the easiest exercises first, through keywords or their synonyms; word by word for the hard ones; move on when stuck. Challenges: a weak vocabulary and a lack of practice. To improve: read more and widely, start with what you like, read something different every day, note new words, read more than once, use the new words.</li>\n<li><strong>Fact</strong>: can be proved true by objective evidence. <strong>Opinion</strong>: a belief, judgement or conclusion that cannot. <strong>Propaganda</strong>: biased or misleading information that promotes a cause; <strong>transfer</strong> links a product or a candidate to something people love (the flag, a film star). <strong>Circular reasoning</strong>: the reason restates the conclusion. <strong>False cause</strong>: B came after A, so A must have caused B.</li>\n<li><strong>Skimming</strong>: a quick look for the overall picture (contents, index, headings, first and last lines, bold and italics, summaries), to preview or review. <strong>Scanning</strong>: a search for one keyword at a time; stop there and read around it.</li>\n<li><strong>SQ3R</strong> (Robinson, <em>Effective Study</em>, 1946): Survey, Question, Read, Recite, Review. <strong>PQRST</strong>: Preview, Question, Read, State (self-recite), Test; Preview and Test for the whole chapter, Question, Read and State for each section.</li>\n<li><strong>Short text</strong>: one key idea; full focus; note how it is introduced, supported and concluded. <strong>Long text</strong>: eat the elephant slice by slice; find your pace, then schedule; notes after every chapter; explain it simply (Feynman).</li>\n<li><strong>A critical reader</strong> (6.5): prepares, keeps an open mind, dwells on the title, learns the terms, writes notes, makes connections, and goes deep, beyond the eagle’s eye view.</li>\n<li><strong>IELTS reading</strong>: 40 questions in 60 minutes; Academic = three long passages; at most 15, 20 and 25 minutes for the three sections; mark a stuck question and move on; read one question, then scan. The lecture’s lists: ordered: missing words, True/False, Yes/No/Not given (except in section 3); not ordered: multiple choice, matching paragraphs, sentence completion, all of section 3 (the official IELTS descriptions put multiple choice and sentence completion in order). Match the following gives the paragraph number. Answers are paraphrased; the same words in an option are often a trap.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Fact or opinion? (a) <em>China is the most populous country in the world.</em> (b) <em>The best treatment for thyroid is homeopathy.</em> (c) <em>IIT Madras is in Chennai.</em> (d) <em>Chennai is the best city in India to live in.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Ask: can it be <strong>checked with objective evidence</strong>?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A <strong>fact</strong>, in the lecture’s sense: data can check it. (Today the data says India has passed China, so the statement is out of date, but it is still a checkable statement, not an opinion.)</li>\n<li>(b) An <strong>opinion</strong>: there is no scientific consensus, and other people prefer allopathy or ayurveda (the lecture’s example).</li>\n<li>(c) A <strong>fact</strong>: anyone can check it on a map.</li>\n<li>(d) An <strong>opinion</strong>: <em>best</em> is a judgement, and people disagree about it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(c) and (d) are my examples.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the error in reasoning or the propaganda technique: (a) <em>This phone is the best because no other phone is better.</em> (b) <em>I wore my blue shirt on the day of the exam and I passed, so the blue shirt brings me luck.</em> (c) An advertisement shows a famous cricketer smiling beside a new motorbike.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Circular reasoning</strong>: “no other phone is better” only says “it is the best” again. No real reason is given.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>False cause</strong>: passing came <em>after</em> wearing the shirt, but it was not <em>caused</em> by the shirt (more likely, by your studying).</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Transfer</strong> (propaganda): the advertiser wants our liking for the cricketer to pass to the motorbike, just as a politician uses a film star or the national flag.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All three are my examples, built on the lecture’s definitions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Skimming or scanning? (a) You read a book’s contents page, introduction and chapter summaries to decide which chapters you need. (b) You run your eyes down a long article looking only for the year in which a scientist won a prize. (c) In a test you read question 1, then look through the passage for its keywords.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Skimming</strong>: you want the overall picture, to <strong>preview</strong> the book.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Scanning</strong>: you look for one specific piece of information, with one keyword in mind (the year).</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Scanning</strong>: you search for the keywords of one question; when you find them, you slow down and read around them.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which step of SQ3R or PQRST is it? (a) Before reading, you look at the title, headings, diagrams and footnotes of a chapter. (b) You turn the heading <em>Causes of the First World War</em> into <em>What caused the First World War?</em> (c) After each section you close the book and say the main ideas aloud. (d) When the chapter is over, you answer all your questions again and rewrite the summaries.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Survey</strong> (SQ3R) or <strong>Preview</strong> (PQRST): a map of the whole chapter before you enter it.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Question</strong>, in both methods: a curious mind absorbs more, like a sponge.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Recite</strong> (SQ3R) or <strong>State, self-recite</strong> (PQRST): recall after each section; if you cannot, go back before moving on.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Review</strong> (SQ3R) or <strong>Test</strong> (PQRST): check the whole chapter, fresh, at the end.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Infer the meaning of the word in bold from its context: <em>The lecture was so <strong>soporific</strong> that half the class fell asleep.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The unknown word is <em>soporific</em>, a describing word for the lecture.</li>\n<li>The clue: <em>so … that half the class fell asleep</em>. The word describes what made the class sleep.</li>\n<li>So <em>soporific</em> must mean something like “making people sleep”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>soporific</em> = making people sleepy (very dull). This is the lecture’s technique for difficult words (6.1); the sentence is mine.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In IELTS reading you have 60 minutes for three sections. (a) How should you divide the time? (b) For which question types can you read question 1, find its answer, and then go on from that point for question 2? (c) What do you do with a question you are stuck on?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) At most <strong>15 minutes</strong> on section 1 (easy), <strong>20</strong> on section 2 and <strong>25</strong> on section 3 (the toughest): 15 + 20 + 25 = 60.</li>\n<li>(b) The <strong>ordered</strong> types: <strong>missing words</strong>, <strong>True/False</strong> and <strong>Yes/No/Not given</strong> (but not Yes/No/Not given in section 3). Multiple choice, matching paragraphs, sentence completion and every question of section 3 are <strong>not ordered</strong>: scan the whole passage. For <em>match the following</em> the paragraph number is given: scan just that paragraph. (This is the lecture’s answer; the official IELTS descriptions also put multiple choice and sentence completion in order.)</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Mark it</strong>, move on, and come back if time is left. The time of 4 or 5 questions spent on one question can cost the marks of 4 or 5 questions.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n"});
